A||||5||hugh||Us vs Them||||6||
Z||000000||hugh||11-22-2001||07:14 AM||||When I was younger I was one of the Us;<BR>I couldn't wait to get my driver's license,I started marking off the days when I had almost a year to go.<BR>I wasn't driving too fast.<BR>Why should I have to be home at your time?I'm old enough to make my own decisions.<BR>They're not going to cover our ballfield with houses,I'll tear up the stakes!<BR>What's wrong with the way I dress?<BR>Then there is the gray area between us and them:<BR>Why are the days so short?I have so much to do.<BR>I'll jump from one trailer roof to the other instead of going down the ladder,it's faster.(work related)<BR>My wife is pregnant!<BR>My wife is pregnant again!<BR>My wife is pregnant,again!<BR>My wife is PREGNANT AGAIN!<BR>MY WIFE IS PREGNANT AGAIN!!!!!<BR>Dad has a nice ring to it!<BR>My kids are growing up too fast!<BR>The house will be paid off soon!<BR>They think their friends know more than I do!Kids sports,activities at school!<BR>Graduations!<BR>Then I became one of them!<BR>Most younger people today don't stop to think that I used to be one of us!<BR>I don't drive as fast(usually).<BR>I'm still old enough to make my own decisions,but I REALLY want to go home early!<BR>What's wrong with the way I dress?<BR>I'm tired,Isn't this day ever going to end?<BR>Climbing up and down this ladder is killing my legs!<BR>Dad,I'm pregnant!<BR>Dad,my wife is pregnant!<BR>I'll spare you all the other pregnant lines(I'm working on number 13).<BR>I've become my father!<BR>I'll finish this up with this statement:<BR>I feel like a young man in an old man's body,my mind has to make allowances for things my body can no longer do.<BR>   WHAT THE HELL HAPPENED?<P>||||reg||6
Z||000001||fogglethorpe||11-22-2001||07:31 AM||||Look on the bright side, Dad...how many men your age build dragsters in their garage, and then race them at the track?<P>But really, I don't know how I'll feel when I get there. I am between the us/them stage now. As I approach forty much more quickly than I'd like to, I feel my body changing, and the way I think also. <P>For my teens, I am one of the "hip" parents...I still go to rock concerts with them, they still appreciate my sense of humor, and their friends tend to think I'm ok...but when my younger boys are teens, I fear I'll be one of the older, out of touch parents.<P>Dad, also, the older I get, the more I realize you were smarter than I thought...||||reg||1
Z||000002||maryjane||11-22-2001||07:54 AM||||Welcome to the club, Hugh. I KNOW it has it's own problems, but being a parent, (grand, & great-grand) is pretty neat too. I can tell without looking at the user-name, just by the text, about how old someone is here. I don't mean offense to any of the younger members either. I remember when having a cool looking car was more important than having a reliable car. When I first met Jane, she was 29 & I was 44. (I'm 52 now)Different tastes in music and everything else. Ran me ragged on both ends for several years.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> Man was I glad when she slowed down a little. <BR>Fogelthorpe, I'm glad to see you make that comment about your dad, especially to him. My father worked me so hard, I forged his name to the enlistment papers, (I was under-age)& joined the military in the middle of Vietnam. But, he laid down the mechanical & work ethic foundation the has carried me thru my whole life. Bar none, he was the BEST auto mechanic (& Ford tractor) I've ever met, & I've met a lot. I remember thinking, in the middle of a straight 8 Packard overhaul: "man, you think you know it all, I'll show you" Now, I realize he really did. <p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 11-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||TRiAD||11-22-2001||10:06 AM||||Wow...I understand the mind making allowances for the body's inabilities...<P>I'm 29, a father of 2, and my 9 year old (yeah, we started young) thinks I'm pretty cool, but my 14 month-old will likely never have the same connection with me that she has. Is there another on the way?!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>You can tell age (usually) by posts, there's a difference in what matters (having cool stuff vs having cool people around you, etc), what doesn't matter (the fast buck vs honesty), and in what motivates you (that weekend party vs taking care of the family). <BR>These are generalizations, but you get the idea. <P>Am I upset about being nearly 30 and not having attained my goals yet? Not really. It's disconcerting, but I'm still truckin', so to speak. <P>So, am I in the grey, or am I getting grey?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||California Kid||11-22-2001||10:15 AM||||Kind of funny how the mind stays young and your body gives you a "wake up call" every now and then!!<P>I'll be hitting 54 this coming summer, haven't got a clue where the time has gone, just seems to be going faster, and faster.<P>It's interesting seeing the younger generation making the same mistakes we did when we were young (they don't listen any more than we did at the time). The learning curve of life can be bitter and sweet!||||reg||1
Z||000005||avengador1||11-22-2001||10:24 AM||||Wait till you hit forty Triad. Im 42 and have a ten year old son (we took our time). My wife says that we act more like brothers than father and son some times. I definitively am more mellow than I used to be. Must be the lowering of my testosterone levels as the add says. I still don't have any grey hairs and I'm glad to report that I'm in a reasonably good shape. Just a little overweight. I think people call this the middle age spread. Anyway I guess my point is you are as young as you feel and age does not mean too much to me anymore.Maybe I'm one of them now. I can't wait to see how I feel at 50. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
